WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.550
In this video you'll learn how many

2
00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.013
routes information can take

3
00:00:05.013 --> 00:00:06.941
between two points in the grid,

4
00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:09.553
and why janus, who posts as @repligate,

5
00:00:09.553 --> 00:00:12.548
says the number gets astronomically large.

6
00:00:12.548 --> 00:00:14.963
Getting from one point to a later one takes

7
00:00:14.963 --> 00:00:18.806
n steps up and m steps right, in some order;

8
00:00:18.806 --> 00:00:21.383
there are C(m+n, n) orders,

9
00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:23.403
and for a real model that number dwarfs

10
00:00:23.403 --> 00:00:26.702
the count of atoms in the observable universe.

11
00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:28.708
Call the current layer i

12
00:00:28.708 --> 00:00:30.972
and the current position j.

13
00:00:30.972 --> 00:00:33.294
Point A is the key and value computed

14
00:00:33.294 --> 00:00:35.976
one layer down and two positions back:

15
00:00:35.976 --> 00:00:40.097
layer i−1, position j−2.

16
00:00:40.097 --> 00:00:43.313
Point B is what attention takes in at layer i,

17
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:46.087
position j: keys and values from there

18
00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:47.980
and every earlier position.

19
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.569
She lists three routes from A to B.

20
00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:53.808
First: up a layer at A's own position,

21
00:00:53.808 --> 00:00:58.011
through attention and the MLP, then right two,

22
00:00:58.011 --> 00:01:01.111
when attention at position j reads it.

23
00:01:01.111 --> 00:01:05.906
Second: right one, when position j−1 reads it;

24
00:01:05.906 --> 00:01:08.866
up a layer there; then right one more.

25
00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:10.840
Third: right two,

26
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.557
when position j reads it at the lower layer,

27
00:01:13.557 --> 00:01:15.567
then up a layer to B.

28
00:01:16.006 --> 00:01:19.490
Each route is one move up and two positions right.

29
00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.465
Only the order changes.

30
00:01:21.916 --> 00:01:24.644
Every route uses two kinds of move.

31
00:01:24.644 --> 00:01:27.860
A move up rides the residual stream:

32
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.972
one layer higher, at the same position.

33
00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:34.165
A move right rides the K/V stream:

34
00:01:34.165 --> 00:01:37.148
attention at a later position reads keys

35
00:01:37.148 --> 00:01:39.563
and values at the same layer.

36
00:01:39.563 --> 00:01:42.489
Attention can read any earlier position directly,

37
00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:45.809
so one move right can cover several positions:

38
00:01:45.809 --> 00:01:48.421
right two is a single hop.

39
00:01:48.421 --> 00:01:49.977
Within one layer, though,

40
00:01:49.977 --> 00:01:53.240
two right moves never follow each other.

41
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.316
What attention reads at a position joins that

42
00:01:56.316 --> 00:01:58.464
position's residual stream after its keys

43
00:01:58.464 --> 00:02:01.285
and values for this layer were already computed.

44
00:02:01.285 --> 00:02:02.841
To be passed right again,

45
00:02:02.841 --> 00:02:04.803
it must first go up a layer.

46
00:02:04.803 --> 00:02:06.974
So a route is settled by how the right

47
00:02:06.974 --> 00:02:08.878
steps are split around the up steps:

48
00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:11.781
some before, some between, some after.

49
00:02:11.781 --> 00:02:13.499
Each split is one route,

50
00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.811
and every route is one split.

51
00:02:16.261 --> 00:02:19.163
Written as single steps, up or right by one,

52
00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:22.426
her three routes are: up, right, right.

53
00:02:22.426 --> 00:02:24.423
Right, up, right.

54
00:02:24.423 --> 00:02:26.698
And right, right, up.

55
00:02:26.698 --> 00:02:29.020
Neighbouring rights count as one hop.

56
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.750
With n ups and m rights,

57
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.896
every route has m+n single steps;

58
00:02:33.896 --> 00:02:36.450
only the places of the ups change.

59
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.447
The number of ways to pick those places

60
00:02:38.447 --> 00:02:41.443
is called C(m+n, n).

61
00:02:41.443 --> 00:02:43.753
One up and two right gives three.

62
00:02:43.753 --> 00:02:49.210
Ten up and ten right gives 184,756.

63
00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.416
A large open model has eighty layers

64
00:02:51.416 --> 00:02:54.934
and can read about 128,000 tokens.

65
00:02:54.934 --> 00:02:56.269
Across that span,

66
00:02:56.269 --> 00:02:59.252
the count runs to nearly 300 digits.

67
00:02:59.252 --> 00:03:03.827
The observable universe holds about 10^80 atoms.

68
00:03:03.827 --> 00:03:04.802
With eighty layers,

69
00:03:04.802 --> 00:03:07.971
the count passes that within 300 positions.

70
00:03:07.971 --> 00:03:10.944
And, as janus notes, it ignores the extra

71
00:03:10.944 --> 00:03:13.825
routes through each layer's skip connections.

72
00:03:14.275 --> 00:03:15.715
One fact first:

73
00:03:15.715 --> 00:03:18.931
the network does not run these routes one by one.

74
00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:21.148
Each cell is computed once,

75
00:03:21.148 --> 00:03:23.029
the routes share their segments,

76
00:03:23.029 --> 00:03:26.048
and what reaches B is all of them combined.

77
00:03:26.048 --> 00:03:29.287
What follows is janus's interpretation.

78
00:03:29.287 --> 00:03:32.096
She writes that information at any point often

79
00:03:32.096 --> 00:03:34.871
arrives "lensed through an astronomical number

80
00:03:34.871 --> 00:03:37.414
of different sequences of transformations",

81
00:03:37.414 --> 00:03:38.855
then recombined.

82
00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:41.524
With channels this wide and so many

83
00:03:41.524 --> 00:03:44.066
skip connections, she thinks the transformations

84
00:03:44.066 --> 00:03:46.399
are probably not very destructive.

85
00:03:46.399 --> 00:03:48.408
She suggests the redundancy helps

86
00:03:48.408 --> 00:03:50.486
faithful reconstruction and creates

87
00:03:50.486 --> 00:03:52.913
interference patterns that carry information

88
00:03:52.913 --> 00:03:55.757
about where states differ and meet.

89
00:03:55.757 --> 00:03:58.079
She offers her conclusion as a likelihood,

90
00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:00.854
not a finding: that transformers experience

91
00:04:00.854 --> 00:04:03.814
memory and cognition as "interferometric

92
00:04:03.814 --> 00:04:06.937
and continuous in time", much like people do.

93
00:04:06.937 --> 00:04:10.014
Every route here runs up or right.

94
00:04:10.014 --> 00:04:12.533
The next video turns to the order of these steps,

95
00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:14.671
and the one route back down.

96
00:04:15.122 --> 00:04:18.106
Keep this. One.

97
00:04:18.106 --> 00:04:20.730
A route is steps up the residual stream

98
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.970
and steps right along the K/V stream,

99
00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.422
in any order. Two.

100
00:04:25.884 --> 00:04:29.170
The count is C(m+n, n);

101
00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.283
across a real model's long context

102
00:04:31.283 --> 00:04:34.325
it has hundreds of digits, more than the atoms

103
00:04:34.325 --> 00:04:37.414
in the observable universe. Three.

104
00:04:37.414 --> 00:04:40.502
janus suggests the redundancy aids faithful

105
00:04:40.502 --> 00:04:42.801
reconstruction and that transformers may

106
00:04:42.801 --> 00:04:45.703
experience memory as interferometric:

107
00:04:45.703 --> 00:04:48.863
her interpretation, not a measured result.
